# 039-skills-gate-scope-create.patch
#
# Local patch -- NOT an upstream PR. Checked 2026-11-04 against upstream main and v0.22.7: the
# skills gate is still a single boolean, so "review new skills but let edits through" is not
# expressible. Nothing upstream tracks it; the nearest issues are about the queue being fed by
# the review fork (#99661 / #146020 / #145377) and PR #99680 (stage-time dedupe, CLOSED
# unmerged), none of which adds a scope knob.
#
# Why: `skills.write_approval: true` gates EVERY skill_manage mutation, including in-place edits
# to a skill that already exists. On a deployment that keeps the gate on precisely to stop the
# background review from inventing new skills out of one-off questions (and to keep memory
# writes reviewed), that means the review's ordinary maintenance patches -- fixing a stale line,
# appending a verified fact -- all queue up for manual approval too. The queue then grows faster
# than it can be drained, and every drain turn is itself a normal turn that can trigger another
# review fork, so approvals produce approvals.
#
# Rejected alternatives, so the next maintainer does not re-try them:
#   * Drain the queue from a 0-LLM cron every N minutes. Works, but the applied patch lands
#     against a stale baseline: anything that touched the same skill in the window either makes
#     the patch fail (it rots in the queue) or overwrites the newer change. The window is also a
#     race by construction.
#   * Turn the gate off (`write_approval: false`). Kills the creation review too, which is the
#     whole reason the gate is on.
#   * Gate by origin (`foreground` vs `background_review`). Wrong axis: what needs review is
#     creating/replacing a skill, not who wrote it -- the review fork also files good edits, and a
#     foreground `create` deserves the same review as a background one.
#   * Keep staging everything and auto-approve the queue. Still leaves a queue, a stale baseline
#     and a second write path; strictly worse than deciding at the gate.
#
# After: `skills.write_approval_scope` (default "all") narrows the skills gate:
#   "all"    -> every skill_manage mutation stages, byte-identical to v0.22.7 (regression-safe,
#               including a missing/invalid/unreadable config value: those resolve to "all").
#   "create" -> `create`, `edit` (whole-SKILL.md rewrite), `delete`, and `write_file`/`remove_file`
#               that target SKILL.md still stage; `patch` and supporting-file writes
#               (references/, scripts/, assets/, templates/) apply immediately.
# Memory is untouched: `memory.write_approval` has no scope knob and keeps staging/inline-prompting
# exactly as before, including the background-review origin.
#
# Wiring: `evaluate_gate()` takes an optional `mutations` list (action/name/file_path per op).
# `skill_manager_tool._apply_skill_write_gate()` builds it (batch -> its operations, flat call ->
# one op) and `_run_write_gate()` forwards it. Callers that pass nothing keep unconditional
# staging, and an unknown action or malformed op fails CLOSED (staged), so a future upstream
# action cannot slip past the gate.
#
# Runtime files only:
#   tools/write_approval.py       (+2 module constants block, +2 helpers, evaluate_gate kwarg)
#   tools/skill_manager_tool.py   (_run_write_gate kwarg, _apply_skill_write_gate op list)
#   hermes_cli/config_defaults.py (the new key + its comment, so `hermes config set` accepts it)
#
# Verified 2026-11-04 on the v0.22.7 tag tree (clone at /tmp/pristine-v0227):
#   * forward apply on pristine: rc=0, zero fuzz, zero .rej/.orig; reverse --dry-run rc=0.
#     No other patch in patches/*.patch touches these three files (grep -l), so pristine ==
#     the lexicographic application order for this patch.
#   * py_compile rc=0 on all three files; scripts/check_structure.py: STRUCTURE OK, GateDecision
#     0 -> 0 methods, +2 intentional top-level helpers (the WARN).
#   * ruff check --config pyproject.toml: "All checks passed!" before and after; ruff format --diff
#     shows none of the added lines as reformat targets.
#   * Behaviour probe (scope=create, gate on, skill "demo-skill" on disk):
#       RED on pristine: 8 assertions fail -- evaluate_gate() has no `mutations` kwarg, and
#         skill_manage(patch) returns staged=true with the file unchanged.
#       GREEN on patched: patch allowed and the file changed (success, no pending id); create
#         staged and NOT written; write_file SKILL.md staged; write_file references/x.md allowed;
#         delete staged; batch [patch, create] staged (one structural op poisons the batch);
#         memory evaluate_gate still staged.
#       scope unset and scope="nonsense": patch still staged on both trees (default preserved).
#
# Re-anchor on a base bump: anchors are `evaluate_gate()` in tools/write_approval.py (signature +
# the `if subsystem == SKILLS or current_origin() == ...` line) and `_apply_skill_write_gate()` /
# `_run_write_gate()` in tools/skill_manager_tool.py. Retire this patch instead of re-anchoring if
# upstream grows a real per-action / per-scope approval configuration (grep config_defaults for
# "write_approval" and check for a scope/actions key) -- then map our value onto theirs and tell
# the user the local key is superseded rather than carrying both.
diff --git a/hermes_cli/config_defaults.py b/hermes_cli/config_defaults.py
index 426b5a9f..9c2ecb13 100644
--- a/hermes_cli/config_defaults.py
+++ b/hermes_cli/config_defaults.py
